{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791611934,"nonce":"QUUfp84r0me0SPAxFK3kljd1M0Q=","title":"A lazy function's first optimisation decodes every library body its callees reach","message":"Each lazily optimised function is optimised in a scratch module that Scratch::reach fills with the function and the transitive closure of its direct callees. Library callees come from the split snapshot, so the closure decodes library bodies down to the generic dict/set/repr machinery behind string.format and tostring, far below anything the inliner could take. Probed on the light Mac build: nbody's lua$chunk brought in 168 functions in 4.3 ms (151 decoded library bodies, 3.3 ms; 17 program bodies, 0.7 ms), method_call's 146 in 3.5 ms. The chunk of a program with a constant-trip loop is compiled before its first call (static-hot), so this sits on the start-up's critical path (28fefa0).\n\nWhat the passes read of a callee other than for inlining are module-wide facts: purity and the error-flag effect. Purity was already recorded on library functions at snapshot build; the error-flag effect was not, and the error-flag summaries were computed once per module from whichever scratch asked first.\n\nFix:\n- reach takes a library callee (not a root) as its shell and does not follow what it calls; the inliner, before each of its own iterations, has the OptCache decode the shells the functions it works on call, when the body is small enough for some call to inline (FnEntry.insts records each body's instruction count, so a large body is judged without decoding it), adding shells for what the decoded body calls.\n- purity counts a shell recorded pure as a pure leaf instead of demoting it.\n- snapshot builds record FunctionAttributes.keeps_error_flag (a call leaves the flag exactly as found), found by the error-flag summaries over the library's bodies with the runtime's flag name (Lua zl_pending, Python py$exc, which a library body never stores); summaries read it for a shell.\n- Split format major 7.\n\nChecks: with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1, the optimised bodies of nbody, method_call, op_overload, binary_trees, mandelbrot, collatz, any_field, branchy, closure_call and lambda_call are byte-identical to before; tokenize, records and pipeline's main bodies differ only in ids and pointer constants (they differ that way between two runs of the old build too). Without the recorded flag effect, tokenize and records each kept one error-flag check after a call (+12 HIR lines); with it they match. The 22 Python speed kernels print what CPython prints.\n\nAfter: the same chunks bring in 37 functions in 1.0 ms (nbody) and 28 in 0.45 ms (method_call).\n\nMeasured on the Mac against the build before (fddcefa4), interleaved:\n- Wall to the first statement, light build, 7 rounds, medians (ms): nbody 54.1 -\u003e 50.3, method_call 66.7 -\u003e 63.7, mandelbrot 25.2 -\u003e 22.7, tokenize 19.1 -\u003e 17.9, pipeline 21.1 -\u003e 19.8, op_overload 17.6 -\u003e 16.6, records 18.6 -\u003e 18.1, fib 11.8 -\u003e 11.3; collatz 11.3 -\u003e 12.2 (its static-hot function is not the chunk).\n- Whole runs, same rounds: nbody 167.6 -\u003e 164.2, method_call 88.9 -\u003e 87.0, mandelbrot 57.6 -\u003e 53.7, records 77.9 -\u003e 72.1, pipeline 363.5 -\u003e 354.5, tokenize 225.6 -\u003e 220.8, op_overload 101.9 -\u003e 100.0, fib 32.5 -\u003e 31.6, collatz 182.3 -\u003e 182.1; output unchanged.\n- Start-up instructions, llvm-backend build, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3: Lua 17 benchmarks 26.95G -\u003e 24.71G (-8.3%; any_field -17.6%, method_call and mandelbrot -11.8%, tokenize -10.5%, op_overload, pipeline and records about -9%, nbody -5.9%); Python 9 kernels 48.03G -\u003e 47.89G (-0.3%; chaos -2.4%).","files":null}]}